==English==
{{was wotd|2022|April|28}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|en|UK}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈfɛ.ləʊ/|a=RP}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈfɛ.lo/|a=Scotland}}
** {{IPA|en|/ˈfɛ.lou/|a=Wales,non-toe-tow}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfɛ.loʊ/|/ˈfɛlə/<a:colloquial,nonstandard,usually spelled "fella">|a=GA,CA}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfe.ləʉ/|a=AU}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈfe.lɐʉ/|a=NZ}}
* {{IPA|en|a=Indic|/ˈfɛ.lːo(ʊ)/|/-ɔʊ/<a:non-toe-tow>}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-fellow.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɛləʊ|s=2}}
* {{hyph|en|fel|low}}
* {{hmp|en|felloe}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*peḱ-|id=livestock|*legʰ-}}

From {{inh|en|enm|felawe,felowe|t=companion, good friend}}<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=fē̆lau(e|pos=n|id=MED15496}}</ref> from {{inh|en|ang|fēolaga}}, from {{der|en|non|félagi}}, derived from {{m|non|félag|t=joint venture; partnership|lit=a laying together of property}}, from {{m|non|fé|t=livestock, property; money}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*peḱ-|t=livestock; wealth|id=livestock}}) + {{m|non|lag|t=something laid down; right position; arrangement; companionship, fellowship; partnership}} (ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*legʰ-|t=to lie down}}).<ref name="OED">{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=69094|date=March 2022|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref>

{{col-top|2|cog}}
* {{cog|non|filaga}}, {{m|non|felaka|pos=accusative singular|t=partner}}, from which the other terms are derived:
* {{cog|da|fælle|t=companion}}
* {{cog|fo|felagi|t=member; partner}}
* {{cog|is|félagi|t=companion, comrade, fellow; member; partner}}
* {{cog|no|felle|t=companion}}
* {{cog|gmq-osw|fälaghi}} (modern {{cog|sv|felaga}}, {{m|sv|felaha|pos=accusative singular|t=partner}})
* {{cog|sco|falow}}, {{m|sco|fallow}}, {{m|sco|follow|t=associate, companion, comrade}}
{{col-bottom}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|chiefly|in the plural|also|figuratively}} A [[companion#Noun|companion]]; a [[comrade#Noun|comrade]].
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:associate}}
#* {{RQ:Calvin Golding Ephesians|chapter=The .XXXIIII. Sermon, which is the Second vppon the Fifth Chapter|folio=245|verso=1
|passage=But now vnderſtand you, that the kingdome of God is communicated vntoo you by our Lord Jeſus Chriſt, bycauſe he hath purchaced it for you, and made you his brethren and '''fellowes'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Timon of Athens|act=IV|scene=ii|page=90|column=1
|passage=[W]e are '''Fellovves''' ſtill, / Seruing alike in ſorrovv: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Sylvester Du Bartas|year=1611|day=4|part=2|pages=559–560|pageref=560
|passage=Rich-men adore their Gold: whoſo aſpires / To lift to Heav'n his ſight and Soules Deſires, / He muſt be Poor (at leaſt-vviſe like the Poor). / Riches and Fear are '''fellovvs''' euer-more.}}
#* {{RQ:King James Version|Judges|11|37|column=1
|passage=And ſhe ſaid vnto her father &#91;{{w|Jephthah}}&#93;, Let this thing be done for me: Let me alone two moneths, that I may goe vp and downe vpon the mountaines, and bewaile my virginitie, I, and my '''fellowes'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Book of Common Prayer|year=1662|chapter=The Psalmes of DAVID, of the Old Translation, Pointed as They Shall be Said or Sung in Churches. &#91;Psal&#91;m&#93; 45.&#93;|url=https://books.google.com/books?id=t8tCG6Tlr60C&pg=RA4-PA4|column=1
|passage=The kings daughter is all glorious vvithin: her clothing is of vvrought gold. She ſhall be brought unto the king in raiment of needle-vvork: the virgins that be her '''fellovvs''' ſhall bear her company, and ſhall be brought unto thee.}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe New Voyage|part=I|page=70
|passage=[T]he Natives quarrelling vvith their Men, upon ſome Rudeneſs offer'd to their VVomen; and they being ſeparated from their '''Fellovvs''', vvere oblig'd to fly, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Canongate 2|volume=II|chapter=IX|page=253
|passage=[T]his dead fellow had been misused by Rothsay and his '''fellows''', who were in the street in mask and revel, stopping men and women, compelling them to dance, or to drink huge quantities of wine, with other follies needless to recount; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|volume=II|chapter=VI|page=116
|passage=One rioter was seized, and ordered by the privy council to be whipped. His '''fellows''' rescued him and beat the hangman.}}
#* {{RQ:Robert Browning Men and Women|volume=II|poem=Old Pictures in Florence|stanza=8|page=34
|passage=When you style them, you of the little wit, / Old Master this and Early the other, / Not dreaming that Old and New are '''fellows''', / That a younger succeeds to an elder brother, / [[w:Leonardo da Vinci|Da Vincis]] derive in good time from [[w:Dello di Niccolò Delli|Dellos]].}}
#* {{RQ:Hough Purchase Price|chapter=The Gateway, and Some Who Passed|page=29
|passage=Carried somehow, somewhither, for some reason, on these surging floods, were these travelers, of errand not wholly obvious to their '''fellows''', yet of such sort as to call into query alike the nature of their errand and their own relations.}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|in the plural}} An [[animal]] which is a [[member#Noun|member]] of a [[breed#Noun|breed]] or [[species]], or a [[flock#Noun|flock]], [[herd#Noun|herd]], etc.
#* {{RQ:Livy Holland Romane Historie|year=1659|book=I|page=6
|passage=But as ſome of the Oxen in driving, miſſed their '''fellovvs''' behind and honing after them, '''bellovved''' as their nature is: ''{{w|Hercules}}'' chanced to heare them lovv again, and anſvver from out of the cave vvherein they had been beſtovved: vvhereat he turned back, and made haſte thither.}}
#* {{RQ:L'Estrange Fables of Aesop|fablenumber=CCCXXX|fable=A Sheep-biter Hang’d|page=287
|passage=A Certain ''Shepherd'' had One ''Favourite-Dog'', that he had a Particular Confidence in above all the reſt. He fed him vvith his Ovvn hand, and took more Care of him, in ſhort, then of any of his '''Fellovvs'''.}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|in the plural}} An [[object#Noun|object]] which is [[associated#Adjective|associated]] with another object; especially, as [[part#Noun|part]] of a [[set#Noun|set]].
#* {{RQ:Homer Pope et al Odyssey|book=III|page=118|lines=382–383
|passage=But five tall barks the vvinds and vvaters toſt / Far from their '''fellovvs''', on th’''Ægyptian'' coaſt.}}
#* {{RQ:Gibbon Roman Empire|volume=VI|chapter=LXVII|page=485
|passage=The great cannon of [[w:Mehmed the Conqueror|Mahomet]] has been ſeparately noticed; an important and viſible object in the hiſtory of the times: but that enormous engine was flanked by two '''fellows''' [''i.e.'', cannons] almoſt of equal magnitude: {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|also|attributively}} One [[comparable#Adjective|comparable]] in [[characteristic#Noun|characteristics]] with another; especially, as [[belong]]ing to the same [[class#Noun|class]] or [[group#Noun|group]].
#: {{co|en|[[my '''fellow''' Americans]]}}
#: {{ux|en|Rebecca and her '''fellow''' workers are to go on strike.}}
#* {{RQ:Middleton Tourneur Revengers Tragaedie|page=13
|passage=Indeed he vvas a vvorthy Gentleman / Had his eſtate beene '''fellovv''' to his mind.}}
#* {{RQ:Bunyan Pilgrim's Progress|edition=5th|pages=85–86|pageref=85
|passage=He that ſhall die there, although his death will be unnatural, and his pain perhaps great, he will yet have the better of his '''fellow'''; not only becauſe he will be arrived at the Cœleſtial City ſooneſt, but becauſe he will eſcape many miſeries that the other will meet with in the reſt of his journey.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:James Francis Hogan|chapter=Preface|chapterurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=xnDOAAAAMAAJ&pg=PP9|title=The Irish in Australia|location=London|publisher=Ward & Downey,{{nb...|12, York Street, Covent Garden.}}|year=1887|oclc=81343284
|passage=It is now some five years since I conceived the idea of writing a history of my '''fellow'''-countrymen in Australasia, but it was only within the last year or two that I could find sufficient time to make any material progress with the undertaking, although I had been collecting the materials for some period in advance.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Mrs. {{w|Arthur Lyttelton}} [''i.e.'', {{w|Kathleen Lyttelton}}]|chapter=Professions|title=Women and Their Work|location=London|publisher=[[w:Methuen Publishing|Methuen & Co.]]{{nb...|36 Essex Street W.C.}}|month=July|year=1901|page=112|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=IwRPAAAAMAAJ&pg=PA112|oclc=2453505
|passage=There are journalists who work for a low rate of pay, just as there are poor women who take in needlework at a cheaper rate than their '''fellows''', and they are alike making life more difficult for other women.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Anna Stilz|chapter=Legitimacy and Self-determination|title=Territorial Sovereignty: A Philosophical Exploration|location=Oxford, Oxfordshire|publisher=w:Oxford University Press|year=2019|section=part II (Self-determination)|page=99|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=rbSmDwAAQBAJ&pg=PA99|isbn=978-0-19-883353-6
|passage=An important part of treating others as independent persons involves respecting them as autonomous deliberators, who can reason for themselves how to act. Yet our imagined neighbor is insensitive to the need to engage her '''fellows''' in this way. She does not offer them any reasons that might lead them to share her point of view about what justice requires, nor does she inquire into, or respond to, their reasons for not sharing it.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Paul Bigland|title=My Weirdest and Wackiest Rover Yet|magazine=[[w:Rail (magazine)|Rail]]|location=Peterborough, Cambridgeshire|publisher=[[w:Bauer Media Group|Bauer Media]]|date=2 December 2020|page=68|issn=0953-4563|oclc=999467860
|passage=My '''fellow''' passengers are a mixture of people returning from a day out in the capital, locals doing short hops, and a few (like me) heading farther afield.}}
#* {{RQ:en:CNN|author=Issy Ronald|title=Kevin, world’s tallest male dog, dies shortly after securing record|url=https://www.cnn.com/2024/06/25/us/worlds-tallest-male-dog-kevin-dies-intl-scli|date=2024-06-25
|passage=Eventually, he was named the world’s tallest male dog, taking over the title from '''fellow''' Great Dane Zeus who also died aged three in September, after suffering from bone cancer.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20260107184400/https://www.cnn.com/2024/06/25/us/worlds-tallest-male-dog-kevin-dies-intl-scli}}
## {{lb|en|chiefly in the negative}} One with [[ability|abilities]], [[achievement]]s, [[skill#Noun|skills]], etc., [[equal#Adjective|equal]] to those of another; a thing with characteristics, [[worth#Noun|worth]], etc., equal to those of another thing.
##: {{co|en|to be without '''fellow'''}}
##: {{co|en|to have no '''fellows'''}}
##* {{RQ:T. More Robinson Utopia|sig=B.i.|verso=1|sigend=B.ii.|page=32
|passage=[I]n reaſonynge, and debatynge of matters what by his naturall witte, & what by daylye exerciſe, ſuerlye he hadde fewe '''fellowes'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|act=III|scene=i|page=119|column=1
|passage=But I am conſtant as the Northerne Starre, / Of vvhoſe true fixt, and reſting quality, / There is no '''fellovv''' in the Firmament.}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Macbeth|act=II|scene=iii|page=137|column=2
|passage=''Macb''[''eth'']. 'Tvvas a rough Night. / ''Len''[''n'']''ox''. My young remembrance cannot paralell / A '''fellovv''' to it.}}
##* {{RQ:De Quevedo L'Estrange Visions|page=258
|passage=There happen'd lately ſo terrible an ''Uproar'', and ''Diſorder'' in ''Hell'', that (though it be a place of perpetual ''Outrage'', and ''Confuſion'') the oldeſt Devil there never knevv the '''Fellovv''' of it; and the ''Inhabitants'' expected nothing leſs then an abſolute ''Topſy-Turvy'', and ''Diſſolution of their Empire''.}}
##* {{RQ:Swift Directions to Servants|chapter=I (Directions to the Butler)|page=122
|passage=Put your finger into every bottle to feel whether it be full, which is the surest way, for feeling has no '''fellow'''.}}
##* {{RQ:Richardson Pamela|volume=I|chapter=Letter XXVIII|page=96
|passage=And here are four other Shifts, one the '''Fellovv''' to that I have on; another pretty good one, and the other tvvo old fine ones, that vvill ſerve me to turn and vvind vvith at home, for they are not vvorth leaving behind me; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Smollett Peregrine Pickle|chapter=Peregrine is Sent to a Boarding-school, Becomes Remarkable for His Genius and Ambition|page=36
|passage=[T]his ſerves to inform you, that Mr. Jennings is gone, and Mr. Keypſtick will never meet with his '''fellow'''.}}
## {{lb|en|chiefly|in the plural}} One in the [[same#Adjective|same]] [[condition#Noun|condition]], or [[situation]] of [[need#Noun|need]], as another.
##* {{RQ:Hobbes Leviathan|chapter=Of the Causes, Generation, and Definition of a Common-wealth|page=87
|passage=[I]rrationall creatures cannot diſtinguiſh betvveene ''Injury'', and ''Dammage''; and therefore as long as they be at eaſe, they are not offended vvith their '''fellovves''': {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Goldsmith Traveller|page=4
|passage=And oft I wiſh, amidſt the ſcene, to find / Some ſpot to real happineſs conſign'd, / Where my worn ſoul, each wand'ring hope at reſt, / May gather bliſs to ſee my '''fellows''' bleſt.}}
##* {{RQ:Byron Mazeppa|stanza=III|lines=51–52|page=7
|passage=For danger levels man and brute, / And all are '''fellows''' in their need.}}
## {{lb|en|archaic|chiefly|in the plural}}
### An [[equal#Noun|equal]] in [[character#Noun|character]], [[power#Noun|power]], [[rank#Noun|rank]], etc.; a [[peer#Noun|peer]].
###* {{RQ:Shakespeare Julius Caesar|act=V|scene=iii|page=129|column=1
|passage=Are yet tvvo Romans liuing ſuch as theſe? / The laſt of all the Romans, far thee vvell: / It is impoſſible, that euer ''Rome'' / Should breed thy '''fellovv'''.}}
###* {{RQ:Tasso Fairfax Godfrey of Bulloigne|book=1|stanza=12|page=3
|passage=[C]hieftaine of the reſt / I choſe him heere, the earth ſhall him allovv, / His '''fellovves''' late, ſhall be his ſubiects novv.}}
### One [[live#Verb|living]] at the same [[time#Noun|time]], or about the same [[age#Noun|age]] as another, especially when in the same [[field#Noun|field]] of [[study#Noun|study]] or [[work#Noun|work]].
# {{n-g|Senses relating to academia; often in the form {{m|en||Fellow}}.}}
## An [[academic#Noun|academic]] who [[constitute]]s an [[educational institution]] (such as a college or university); now particularly, a [[senior#Adjective|senior]] member [[involve]]d in [[teaching#Noun|teaching]], [[research#Noun|research]]{{,}} and [[management]] of the institution.
##* {{RQ:Fuller Church History|book=X|section=III|sectionname=To [[w:Thomas Dacres (younger)|Thomæ Dacres]] de Chesthunt, Armig:|subsection=19 (An Act for Chelsey-Colledge)|page=50
|passage=In the Parliament novv ſitting at ''VVeſtminſter'' (in vvhoſe parallel Convocation nothing of conſequence) the moſt remarkable thing Enacted vvas, the Act made to enable the ''Provoſt'', and '''''Fellovves''' of Chelsey-Colledge, to dig a trench'' out of the river ''Lee'', {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Wood Athenae Oxonienses|volume=I|entry=[[w:John Stanbridge|JOHN STANBRIDGE]]|column=17
|passage=One ''Thomas Stanbridge'', {{...}} vvho dying 1522, left ſeveral Books to the Coll. of vvhich he had been '''Fellovv'''; vvhich, if I miſtake not, vvas ''Magd''[''alen''] College.}}
##* {{RQ:Macaulay History of England|volume=II|chapter=VIII|page=289
|passage=The '''fellows''' [of Magdalene College, Cambridge] were, by the statutes which their founder had drawn up, empowered to select their own president from among persons who were, or had been, '''fellows''' either of their society or of New College.}}
## A (senior) member of a [[learned#Adjective|learned]] or [[professional#Adjective|professional]] [[society]].
##: {{ux|en|a '''Fellow''' of the Royal Society}}
##* {{RQ:Evelyn Diary|volume=I|entrydate=6 January 1661|page=329
|passage=I was now chosen (and nominated by his Majestie for one of y{{sup|e}} Council) by suffrage of the rest of y{{sup|e}} Members, a '''Fellow''' of y{{sup|e}} Philosophic Society now meeting at Gresham College, where was an assembly of divers learning gentlemen.}}
### {{lb|en|specifically|Britain|historical}} A senior member of an [[Inn of Court]].
###* {{RQ:Wood Athenae Oxonienses|volume=I|entry=WILLIAM BLANDIE|columns=147–148|columnref=147
|passage=About the ſame time he vvent to ''London'', became '''Fellovv''' of the ''Middle Temple'', and aftervvards tranſlated from Lat. to Engliſh ''The five Books of Hieronimus Oſorius, containing a diſcourſe of Civil and Chriſtian Nobility'', Lond. 1576. qu[arto].}}
## {{n-g|An [[honorary]] [[title#Noun|title]] [[bestow]]ed by an educational institution upon a [[distinguished#Adjective|distinguished]] person (often an [[alumna]] or [[alumnus]]).}}
## {{lb|en|Britain}} A [[scholar]] [[appoint]]ed to a [[fellowship]], that is, a [[paid#Adjective|paid]] [[academic#Adjective|academic]] [[position#Noun|position]] [[hold#Verb|held]] for a certain [[period]] which usually [[require]]s the scholar to [[conduct#Verb|conduct]] research.
## {{lb|en|Canada|US}} A [[physician]] [[undergo]]ing a fellowship ([[supervised#Adjective|supervised]] [[subspecialty]] [[medical#Adjective|medical]] [[training#Noun|training]]) after having [[complete#Verb|completed]] a [[residency]] ([[specialty]] training program).
## {{lb|en|US}} A member of a college or university who [[manage]]s its [[business]] [[interest#Noun|interests]].
## {{lb|en|US}} A senior [[researcher]] or [[technician]] in a [[corporation]], especially one [[engage]]d in research and [[development]].
# {{lb|en|informal}} A [[male#Adjective|male]] person; a [[bloke]], a [[chap#Noun|chap]], a [[guy#Noun|guy]], a [[man#Noun|man]]; also, {{n-g|preceded by a modifying word, sometimes with a [[sense#Noun|sense]] of [[mild#Adjective|mild]] [[reproach#Noun|reproach]]}}: {{n-g|used as a [[familiar#Adjective|familiar]] [[term#Noun|term]] of [[address#Noun|address]] to a man}}.
#: {{syn|en|Thesaurus:man}}
#: {{co|en|[[my dear '''fellow''']]}}
#: {{co|en|old '''fellow'''}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Timon of Athens|act=I|scene=i|page=82|column=1
|passage=Looke in thy laſt vvorke, vvhere thou haſt fegin'd{{sic|feign'd}} him a vvorthy '''Fellovv'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Hall Recollection|title=Farewell Sermon|page=687
|passage=[H]ovv happy ſhall vve be, if each of vs ſhal haue God to go vvith him! Certainely (my deare '''fellovves''') vve ſhall neuer complaine of the vvant of Maiſters, of friends, vvhile vve finde our ſelues ſure of him; nothing can make vs miſerable vvhile vve are furniſhed vvith him.}}
#* {{RQ:Bunyan Pilgrim's Progress|page=105
|passage=He is the Son of one ''Sayvvell'', he dvvelt in ''Prating-rovv''; and he is knovvn of all that are acquainted with him, by the name of ''Talkative'' in ''Prating-rovv''; and notvvithſtanding his fine tongue, he is but a ſorry '''fellovv'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Spectator|author=Steele|issue=48|date=25 April 1711|page=312
|passage=I am an old '''fellow''', and extremely troubled with the gout; but having always a strong vanity towards being pleasing in the eyes of women, I never have a moment's ease, but I am mounted in high-heeled shoes, with a glazed wax-leather instep.}}
#* {{RQ:Fielding Tom Jones|volume=IV|book=XII|chapter=Containing a Remark or Two of Our Own, and Many More of the Good Company Assembled in the Kitchin|page=240
|passage=[Y]ou don't knovv vvhat a Devil of a '''Fellovv''' he is. He can take me up vvith one Hand, and throvv me out at VVindow, {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Hume Morals|page=163
|passage=Thus, I have obſerv'd, that, amongſt the ''French'', the firſt Queſtions, with regard to a Stranger, are, ''Is he polite? Has he Wit?'' In our own Country, the chief Praiſe beſtow'd is always that of a ''good-natur'd, ſenſible '''Fellow'''''.}}
#* {{RQ:Sterne Sentimental Journey|volume=I|chapter=The Case of Conscience. Paris.|page=111
|passage=Then I ſhall let him ſee I knovv he is a dirty '''fellovv'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Marryat Midshipman|volume=II|page=124
|passage=My good '''fellow''', we did not come here. Destiny brought us in a cart. She may take us to Tyburn in the same way.}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Christmas Carol|pages=163–164
|passage="A merry Christmas, Bob!" said Scrooge, with an earnestness that could not be mistaken, as he clapped him on the back. "A merrier Christmas, Bob, my good '''fellow''', than I have given you, for many a year!{{nb...}}"}}
#* {{quote-book|en|author=Cuthbert Bede [pseudonym; [[w:Edward Bradley (writer)|Edward Bradley]]]|chapter=Mr. Verdant Green’s Morning Reflections are Not so Pleasant as His Evening Diversions|title=[[w:The Adventures of Mr. Verdant Green|The Adventures of Mr. Verdant Green, an Oxford Freshman]]|location=London|publisher={{w|Nathaniel Cooke}}, (late Ingram, Cooke, and Co.){{nb...|Milford House, Strand.}}|year=1853|page=70|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=uJZhAAAAcAAJ&pg=PA70|oclc=1079210034
|passage=["{{w|For He's a Jolly Good Fellow}}"] For he's a jolly good '''fellow'''! / For he's a jolly good '''fellow'''!! / For he's a jolly good '''f-e-e-ell-ow'''!!! / Which nobody can deny!}}
#* {{RQ:Hughes Tom Brown at Oxford|chapter=The First Bump|page=207
|passage="Don't think about it, old '''fellow'''; that's your best plan." / "But I can't think of any thing else," said Tom. "What the deuce is the good of telling a '''fellow''' not to think about it?"}}
#* {{RQ:Trollope Prime Minister|volume=II|chapter=The Duchess's New Swan|page=5
|passage=I think he's a very pleasant '''fellow''', and I'm sure he's a rising man.}}
#* {{RQ:Saki Reginald in Russia|story=The Strategist|page=85
|passage="There'll be about ten girls," speculated Rollo, as he drove to the function, "and I suppose four '''fellows''', unless the Wrotsleys bring their cousin, which Heaven forbid. That would mean Jack and me agains three of them."}}
#* {{RQ:Maxwell Mirror and the Lamp|chapter=XII|page=85
|passage=By the way, Hopkins, let this go no further. We don't want the tale running round that a rich person has arrived. Churchill, my dear '''fellow''', we have such greedy sharks, and wolves in lamb's clothing.}}
#* {{RQ:Faulkner As I Lay Dying|year=1980|chapter=Tull|page=58
|passage=Now and then a '''fellow''' gets to thinking about it. Not often, though. Which is a good thing.}}
#* {{quote-av|en|date=1996-04-14|writer=w:Bill Oakley|actor=w:Hank Azaria|role=w:Superintendent Chalmers|title=w:The Simpsons|episode=w:22 Short Films About Springfield|season=[[w:The Simpsons season 7|7]]|number=21|note=segment "{{w|Skinner & The Superintendent}}", production code 3F18|time=02:37
|passage=Well, Seymour, you are an odd '''fellow''', but I must say you steam a good ham.}}
# {{senseid|en|Q5}} An [[individual#Noun|individual]] [[regardless]] of [[gender#Noun|gender]]; {{n-g|usually [[qualify|qualified]] by an {{lg|adjective}} or used in the plural}}.
#* {{RQ:Dickens Old Curiosity Shop|chapter=35|page=298
|passage=[H]e would sometimes reward her with a hearty slap on the back, and protest that she was a devilish good '''fellow''', a jolly dog, and so forth; all of which compliments Miss Sally would receive in entire good part and with perfect satisfaction.}}
